As far as I know, the only known way to prove that $M_{g,n}$ has no non-trivial automorphisms is by using the fact that the mapping class group of a surface of genus $g$ is finite (by a theorem of Dehn). I would be very interested if there was an alternate proof.

-
That's a good point. It seems like the moduli space should have some more intrinsic reason for not having nontrivial automorphisms. – Kevin H. Lin Jun 25 '10 at 3:53
The mapping class group is infinite for g>1, but still finite-index in the diffeomorphism group, which is what you'd need for an automorphism of M_g,n. – Akhil Mathew Jun 25 '10 at 4:04
@Akhil: I think you're confusing the mapping class group (which is finite) with the diffeomorphism group. – Andy Putman Jun 25 '10 at 4:17
@Andy: Yes, that's true. I was thinking of the Teichmuller modular group, which is indeed finite-index in the diffeomorphism group. – Akhil Mathew Jun 25 '10 at 11:59
@Kevin: I think that the only proof is the one that uses the finite generation of the mapping class group (the only proof I know of that the mapping class group is finite for genus >1 is by showing that it is finitely generated and then using the fact that a finitely generated group is Hopfian if and only if it is finite). – Andy Putman Jun 25 '10 at 15:24

