``` Yes, you can do that, and you don't need to put `func` into the base class. 

You could do something like this:

```
class A {
public:
    void func() { }
};

class B : public A {
public:
    void func() { // now you can call func from both classes
        A::func();
    }
};
``` 

Or you could just leave out the `A::` prefix:

```
class A {
public:
    void func() { }
};

class B : public A {
public:
    void func() {
        func();
    }
};
``` 

The `A::` prefix tells the compiler to look for the function definition inside the `A` class, so by leaving it out, you're telling the compiler to look elsewhere. The compiler will find the function in the current scope (which is `B`). You can also leave out the `func()` arguments since the compiler knows which function you are referring to. 

You can also define the function in the header file as a static member function and then call it using the class name:

```
class A {
public:
    static void func() { }
};

class B : public A {
public:
    void func() {
        A::func();
    }
};
``` 

This way, you don't need to create an instance of `A` to call `func`, you can call it directly through the class name. This method is usually used when you want to call the function without creating an instance of the class or when you want to avoid creating a new instance each time you call the function. 

Note: All of these examples assume that the function `func()` does not take any arguments. If it does, you'll need to pass the arguments in the function call. Also, please make sure that the function `func()` is declared as `public` in its class, otherwise you won't be able to access it from other classes. 

Lastly, if you're going to be calling `func()` from both classes often, it might be more efficient to move `func()` to a base class and inherit from it. This way, you only have to define the function once and both classes can use it. 

