Given that a pair's sum is even if both numbers in the pair are either odd or even, we can count the number of odd and even numbers in the set separately. The total number of pairs that satisfy the condition will be the product of the counts of odd and even numbers (since each odd number can pair with any even number) plus half the square of the count of even numbers (because each even number can pair with any other even number, but we divide by 2 to avoid double counting).

